WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:20.360
A total disregard for his own personal safety,
an indomitable fighting spirit, reckless courage,

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:22.970
… and the gift of being nearly indestructible.

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:28.071
This soldier survived five wars, including
World War I and World War II becoming one

00:00:28.071 --> 00:00:33.320
of the most decorated British officers of
all time and sustaining countless injuries.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:39.300
Was he the unluckiest soldier of all time
or the quintessential British war hero?

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:45.370
Make up your mind as you hear the story of
today’s protagonist: Adrian Carton de Wiart,

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:48.510
the unbreakable soldier.

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:52.720
Typical Toff?

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:58.309
Adrian Carton de Wiart was born into an aristocratic
family in Brussels, Belgium on 5 May 1880.

00:00:58.309 --> 00:01:02.750
The father of this quintessential British
war hero was actually Belgian, and a lawyer.

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:05.129
The mother of this quintessential British
war hero, was actually Irish.

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:09.200
Adrian was so Belgian in fact that throughout
his life he was amused by the rumour that

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.590
he was an illegitimate child of King Leopold
II, he who owned the Congo.

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:17.640
Adrian’s mother sadly passed away when he
was just six and his father Léon moved the

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.009
family to Cairo, Egypt.

00:01:20.009 --> 00:01:23.869
During this period Léon married again and
Adrian’s stepmother thought it good for

00:01:23.869 --> 00:01:27.700
the boy’s education to spend some time in
a British boarding school.

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:32.210
As the family was Catholic, he was sent to
a Catholic school, The Oratory, near Birmingham.

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:35.259
The founder of the school, Cardinal John Henry
Newman, once wrote

00:01:35.259 --> 00:01:41.070
“Fear not that thy life shall come to an
end, but rather fear that it shall never have

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:44.929
a beginning”[TA2]
Adrian’s life would be on the brink of coming

00:01:44.929 --> 00:01:48.609
to an end countless times, but he never showed
fear.

00:01:48.609 --> 00:01:53.420
And for sure his life did have one hell of
a beginning.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:57.939
By 1899 Adrian was 19 and attending Balliol
College at Oxford.

00:01:57.939 --> 00:02:01.899
At this stage in his life – as per his father’s
desire – he could have easily settled for

00:02:01.899 --> 00:02:07.609
a law degree and the pleasant existence of
a typical ‘toff’, enjoying his family’s

00:02:07.609 --> 00:02:08.609
wealth.

00:02:08.609 --> 00:02:10.119
Except he didn’t.

00:02:10.119 --> 00:02:19.100
‘War was in my blood’
On the 11th of October 1899 the 2nd Boer War

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:23.930
broke out, pitting the British Empire against
the Orange Free State and the Transvaal Republic

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:26.549
in South Africa [TA3]
This was a revelation for Adrian.

00:02:26.549 --> 00:02:29.019
In his autobiography “Happy Odyssey”,
he wrote

00:02:29.019 --> 00:02:32.660
“At that moment, I knew once and for all
that war was in my blood.

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:36.810
If the British didn’t fancy me, I would
offer myself to the Boers.”

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:39.330
It turns out, the Brits, they did fancy him.

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:43.680
Adrian enrolled as a volunteer faking his
identity as well as his age.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.319
He was soon dispatched to South Africa, while
his father still believed him to be studying

00:02:47.319 --> 00:02:48.959
law at Oxford.

00:02:48.959 --> 00:02:53.280
This was the start of his real life, a soldier’s
life.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:57.360
Very early on, straight after training in
South Africa, De Wiart and his unit were crossing

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:02.959
a river in full view of some Boer Kommandos
when he was shot twice, in the stomach and

00:03:02.959 --> 00:03:03.959
groin.

00:03:03.959 --> 00:03:05.140
[Editing note: two ‘tallies’ appear with
a ping at the bottom of the screen]

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:08.490
We are going to help you out here and keep
count of how many times De Wiart was shot,

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:10.680
‘shrapnell-ed’ or otherwise injured.

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:15.270
After being shot, a superior bluntly asked
if there were many Boers about, to which he

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:18.900
coolly replied
“No, but the few were very good shots”.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:22.629
At the military hospital his true identity
was discovered and so he wasforced to return

00:03:22.629 --> 00:03:27.620
to England to recover - and to face his father’s
wrath, who by now had discovered what his

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:28.970
son was up to.

00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:34.049
Luckily, Léon consented that his son pursued
a military career and after Adrian recovered,

00:03:34.049 --> 00:03:38.120
he decided to quit Oxford for good and to
return to South Africa in 1901 for another

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:39.269
round of fighting.

00:03:39.269 --> 00:03:44.290
This time he did it in style, booking himself
in 1st Class and handing generous tips to

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:50.680
the bar staff, so that he landed in Cape Town
“with exactly one pound in my pocket”

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:53.600
After joining a cavalry regiment De Wiart
was promoted to corporal.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:57.510
This promotion lasted exactly 24 hours, after
which was demoted again to simple trooper

00:03:57.510 --> 00:03:59.770
for threatening to hit a sergeant.

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:04.260
But it was clear that De Wiart was destined
to greater things and after a few months he

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:08.730
had earned – or more probably, bought – a
commission as a 2nd lieutenant.

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:15.209
During the rest of 1901 and until the end
of the 2nd Boer War in 1902, De Wiart greatly

00:04:15.209 --> 00:04:19.769
suffered for the lack of action: his only
opportunity to do anything of military value

00:04:19.769 --> 00:04:25.680
was to cut some barbed wire in a Boer camp,
but even then he was ordered not to do it.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:29.040
Fretting for action, at the end of the war
he applied to be sent to the British Somaliland

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:32.660
to join the hunt for Mohammed bin Adbullah,
also known as the “Mad Mullah”.

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:37.290
Bin Abdullah had started a guerrilla campaign
against the British East African colony, he

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:42.820
was fighting for independence and was considered
by his followers a Sunni holy man, almost

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:47.230
a messiah.[TA4] But to colonial authorities,
he wasn’t the messiah – he was just a

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:50.850
naughty boy[TA5] .
Again, De Wiart’s superiors curbed his fighting

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:55.780
enthusiasm and he was gazetted to a cavalry
regiment in Muttra, India.

00:04:55.780 --> 00:05:01.220
The Indian stay was peaceful and largely uneventful,
except for De Wiart finding a great passion

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:05.640
for ‘pig-stcking’- which basically consists
in chasing wild boar on horseback through

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:07.460
the forest to pierce them with a long spear.

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:08.460
I call this “pork skewers” and I can get
them from my butcher’s without the faff,

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:09.460
but I guess it’s only half the fun.

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:11.640
Anyhow, when chasing one of these pigs in
the jungle, De Wiart fell off the horse.

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:13.530
And the horse fell on him.

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:15.870
He cracked several ribs and one ankle.

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:18.260
[injury tally increases by one]
During his convalescence he had an argument

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:21.200
with a local servant and pelted him with stones.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:25.450
When the servant got out of range, De Wiart
shot him - on the butt.

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:29.510
Seriously, this guy was in dire need of a
battle.

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:33.800
His superiors were not amused by his attack
on the servant and jailed him for a time,

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:36.120
but he managed to avoid demotion.

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:44.410
War on the horizon
In 1904 De Wiart was transferred back to South

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:45.410
Africa.

00:05:45.410 --> 00:05:48.560
In his memoirs he wrote that he was happy
not to see India again.

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:51.880
I guess the feeling was mutual.

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:56.040
The ensuing years were blissful: De Wiart
spent his time gambling, socialising, racing

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:57.870
horses and shooting wildlife.

00:05:57.870 --> 00:06:02.240
He enjoyed several “Liquid meals” as he
called them, but also cultivated his physical

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:03.240
fitness.

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:07.430
At one point he was so strong that he could
rip in half a pack of cards.

00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:12.940
In 1907 De Wiart swore his oath of Allegiance
to the King, formally becoming a British Citizen

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:14.340
within the British Army.

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:18.470
In 1908 De Wiart returned to England, where
his few regimental duties gave him time to

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:23.640
play polo and travel the continent and especially
Austria-Hungary to shoot at grouse, pheasants

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:24.650
and other game.

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:28.720
In the same year his connection to that Empire
was strengthened by his marriage to Friederike

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:33.630
Maria Karoline Henriette Rosa Sabina Franziska
Fugger von Babenhausen[TA6] . And no, he had

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:37.720
not become a Mormon and married seven different
women, this was the full name of the bride,

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:38.910
an Austrian Countess.

00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:41.330
They went on to have two daughters.

00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:45.750
In his memoirs De Wiart recalls the atmosphere
of mistrust at German border posts whenever

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:49.970
he entered the country from France, and how
his French sounding surname attracted suspicion:

00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:54.890
a clear sign that tension was mounting, and
war was on the horizon.

00:06:54.890 --> 00:07:00.910
When WWI broke out, De Wiart was on his way
to British Somaliland to take part in what

00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:05.730
is now mostly a forgotten conflict, the colonial
war against the “Mad Mullah”, whom we

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:06.730
have introduced earlier.

00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:10.750
De Wiart’s desire to go and fight there
had been fulfilled, but upon learning that

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:13.410
the UK had declared war on Germany De Wiart
felt like

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:17.440
“playing in a village cricket match instead
of in the Test”.

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:21.850
For our friend in the US: the Test is to cricket
what the World Series is to baseball.

00:07:21.850 --> 00:07:26.150
De Wiart was assigned to lead a squadron of
the Somaliland Camel Corps on the hunt for

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:28.430
the Mad Mullah’s followers, the Dervishes.

00:07:28.430 --> 00:07:32.660
When tasked with storming an enemy fort, De
Wiart could not resist going on the attack

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:33.660
head first.

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:37.850
But the enemy position was well defended and
he was shot by the Dervishes.

00:07:37.850 --> 00:07:39.390
Three times.

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:40.610
In the face.

00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:44.070
One bullet took away part of his left ear,
a second bullet got him in the left eye, a

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:47.070
third bullet ricocheted and hit him across
the very same eye!

00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:49.570
[Injury tally increases by three]
Of course, De Wiart lost his eye.

00:07:49.570 --> 00:07:50.810
But his assessment of the battle?

00:07:50.810 --> 00:07:53.380
“It had all been most exhilarating fun!”

00:07:53.380 --> 00:07:58.140
De Wiart took the occasion to return to England
where he hoped to be reassigned to service

00:07:58.140 --> 00:07:59.190
on the Western Front.

00:07:59.190 --> 00:08:02.300
The Army medical commission agreed to it,
provided that he use a glass eye.

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:03.840
Which he hated.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:07.500
While riding a cab in London and finding the
glass eye too uncomfortable, he just yanked

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:45.730
it off and threw it from

00:08:45.730 --> 00:08:50.150
the taxi’s window!

00:08:50.150 --> 00:09:05.360
That’s what

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:24.050
I call Fl-eye tipping.

00:09:24.050 --> 00:10:14.700
[Subtitle on screen: Fl-eye tipping]
From then on, he would wear his distinctive

00:10:14.700 --> 00:10:18.079
eye-patch.

00:10:18.079 --> 00:10:27.730
The Western Front
In February 1915 Adrian Carton De Wiart was

00:10:27.730 --> 00:10:34.000
bound for the Western Front, to join a cavalry
regiment, the 4th Royal Irish Dragoon Guards.

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:39.060
His first major engagement was during the
Second Battle of Ypres, in April of that year.

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:43.069
While on March to relieve an infantry unit,
his regiment came under fire from a German

00:10:43.069 --> 00:10:44.069
artillery position.

00:10:44.069 --> 00:10:48.769
A combination of shrapnel and splinters from
his own wristwatch mangled his left hand,

00:10:48.769 --> 00:10:51.980
leaving only part of the palm and two dangling
fingers.

00:10:51.980 --> 00:10:54.690
[injury tally +1]
When the field doctor hesitated to amputate

00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:56.910
them, De Wiart just tore off the two fingers.

00:10:56.910 --> 00:10:59.649
What remained of his hand would be amputated
later in the year.

00:10:59.649 --> 00:11:03.029
At this stage Adrian Carton De Wiart could
have easily asked to be repatriated and leave

00:11:03.029 --> 00:11:05.380
a quiet life behind a desk.

00:11:05.380 --> 00:11:06.380
Except he didn’t.

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:10.910
De Wiart could not give two hoots about his
injuries and he just got on with it, returning

00:11:10.910 --> 00:11:13.220
to battle undeterred and unimpaired.

00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:14.670
‘Tis but a scratch!

00:11:14.670 --> 00:11:18.829
[TA7] He probably said to himself before coming
back for more.

00:11:18.829 --> 00:11:22.930
He went on to fight in the Battle of the Somme,
during which his men recall him pulling pins

00:11:22.930 --> 00:11:28.040
from grenades with his teeth and then flinging
them with his one good hand into enemy territory.

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:31.560
He distinguished himself during the assault
on the village of La Boisselle, France in

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:32.890
1916.

00:11:32.890 --> 00:11:36.519
Three unit commanders from the 8th Battalion
Gloucestershire Regiment had been killed.

00:11:36.519 --> 00:11:38.759
Carton de Wiart then took charge of all three
units.

00:11:38.759 --> 00:11:44.260
With no availability of field radios, field
telephones, nor even pigeons to communicate

00:11:44.260 --> 00:11:48.389
orders, he decided to be his own messenger
and ran back and forth among the three units

00:11:48.389 --> 00:11:52.699
to relay his orders, defying the hail of bullets
and artillery shells raining onto no man’s

00:11:52.699 --> 00:11:53.699
land.

00:11:53.699 --> 00:11:56.639
He and his men managed to hold back the advancing
Germans.

00:11:56.639 --> 00:11:59.709
After this action, De Wiart received the Victoria
Cross

00:11:59.709 --> 00:12:03.779
“For most conspicuous bravery, coolness,
and determination during severe operations

00:12:03.779 --> 00:12:08.149
of prolonged nature”
After La Boisselle, De Wiart continue to lead

00:12:08.149 --> 00:12:11.449
from the front battle after battle, exposing
himself to further injuries.

00:12:11.449 --> 00:12:16.930
For example, in the trenches of Delville Wood,
nicknamed The Devil’s Wood he was shot in

00:12:16.930 --> 00:12:17.930
the head.

00:12:17.930 --> 00:12:18.930
[tally up one.

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:20.370
I lost count]
A clean, straight shot through his skull,

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:21.701
at the back of the head.

00:12:21.701 --> 00:12:24.389
If he had to die, this was the right occasion
to do so.

00:12:24.389 --> 00:12:25.389
Except he didn’t.

00:12:25.389 --> 00:12:29.830
Because De Wiart could not give a damn about
being dead, so he just survived and got on

00:12:29.830 --> 00:12:30.830
with it.

00:12:30.830 --> 00:12:34.370
During the three subsequent battles, he was
shot in the ankle, hip, leg and ear!

00:12:34.370 --> 00:12:35.370
[ping ping ping ping – up 4!]

00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:40.999
But after every stay in hospital he regained
full mobility and returned to the front, time

00:12:40.999 --> 00:12:41.999
after time.

00:12:41.999 --> 00:12:45.899
De Wiart’s assessment of this gruelling
experience, of the meat grinder of the Western

00:12:45.899 --> 00:12:54.939
front was
“Frankly, I enjoyed the war!”

00:12:54.939 --> 00:12:57.020
The Polish Gentleman
The years between the end of WWI and the start

00:12:57.020 --> 00:13:02.480
of WWII are wrongly labelled “inter-war”
years, while this period also saw plenty of

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:04.069
conflicts taking place in Europe.

00:13:04.069 --> 00:13:09.550
And where there was conflict, you’d be sure
to find Adrian Carton de Wiart having the

00:13:09.550 --> 00:13:11.300
time of his life.

00:13:11.300 --> 00:13:15.389
In 1919 De Wiart was assigned to Poland as
a military advisor to the newly independent

00:13:15.389 --> 00:13:20.850
Country, which over a period of two years
found itself at war with Soviet Russia, Czechoslovakia,

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:22.050
Ukraine and Lithuania.

00:13:22.050 --> 00:13:27.680
De Wiart made himself indispensable to Field
Marshall Josef Pilsudski and as usual got

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:29.689
involved in all types of risky situations.

00:13:29.689 --> 00:13:34.680
On the same year, 1919, he survived not one,
but two plane crashes

00:13:34.680 --> 00:13:35.680
[two plane icons ping on screen.

00:13:35.680 --> 00:13:37.250
We will need to keep count also of these]
The second of which resulting in a brief period

00:13:37.250 --> 00:13:38.749
of captivity with the Lithuanians.

00:13:38.749 --> 00:13:42.509
After his release he increased his efforts
to support the Polish military, even organising

00:13:42.509 --> 00:13:45.260
a gun smuggling operation via Hungary.

00:13:45.260 --> 00:13:49.410
In 1920 he got closer to the front as the
war with Russia intensified.

00:13:49.410 --> 00:13:53.509
One day his observation train was attacked
by a charge of Cossack cavalry, with the intent

00:13:53.509 --> 00:13:54.589
of hijacking it.

00:13:54.589 --> 00:13:58.649
He confronted the attackers single-handedly
– quite literally – with his revolver.

00:13:58.649 --> 00:14:02.990
At one point he even fell off the moving train,
but quickly leapt back on it and continued

00:14:02.990 --> 00:14:05.230
the firefight until the train escaped from
the Cossacks.

00:14:05.230 --> 00:14:09.949
When the Polish wars finally ended in victory
in 1921, De Wiart decided to stay in Poland,

00:14:09.949 --> 00:14:14.110
as he had grown fond of its people and its
wildlife – which he proceeded to shoot at

00:14:14.110 --> 00:14:15.259
for the next 18 years.

00:14:15.259 --> 00:14:17.279
The wildlife that is, not the people.

00:14:17.279 --> 00:14:21.760
In 1923 he finally decided to retire, having
now achieved the rank of Major General.

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:26.259
After a life of adventures, this gentleman
would go on to enjoy a quiet and wealthy life

00:14:26.259 --> 00:14:28.390
in his adopted country.

00:14:28.390 --> 00:14:37.779
Except he didn’t
We all know what happened on the 1st of September

00:14:37.779 --> 00:14:38.779
1939.

00:14:38.779 --> 00:14:42.790
In a matter of three weeks Germany invaded
from the West, the Soviet Union from the East

00:14:42.790 --> 00:14:45.149
and the UK and France declared war on the
Axis.

00:14:45.149 --> 00:14:48.439
De Wiart’s quiet life in Poland was no more.

00:14:48.439 --> 00:14:52.110
As a former British military adviser the best
course of action was to flee immediately.

00:14:52.110 --> 00:14:56.740
He abandoned his estate, acquired a fake passport
and crossed the border with Romania.

00:14:56.740 --> 00:14:59.360
Upon returning to the UK De Wiart re-enlisted
in the military.

00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:04.570
In April 1940 De Wiart was recalled into active
service, despite - I should stress again - in

00:15:04.570 --> 00:15:09.269
case you have forgotten - being sixty, one-eyed,
and an amputee.

00:15:09.269 --> 00:15:13.570
He was given the rank of acting Lieutenant-General
and tasked with leading an Anglo-French landing

00:15:13.570 --> 00:15:16.579
force in Norway to halt the German invasion.

00:15:16.579 --> 00:15:19.269
This operation was a complete disaster from
the start.

00:15:19.269 --> 00:15:24.040
While arriving in Norway via seaplane, De
Wiart’s aircraft was forced to crash land

00:15:24.040 --> 00:15:26.600
[third airplane icon appears]
on a fjord, when it was attacked by a German

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:27.630
fighter plane.

00:15:27.630 --> 00:15:31.610
Rather than boarding a rubber dinghy to reach
shore and become a sitting duck, he calmly

00:15:31.610 --> 00:15:35.970
waited in the wreckage until the enemy plane
ran out of ammo and left.

00:15:35.970 --> 00:15:40.670
After reaching his forces by the Trondheim
Fjord De Wiart realised that they were undersupplied,

00:15:40.670 --> 00:15:43.759
undergunned, and soon to be encircled by the
Germans.

00:15:43.759 --> 00:15:48.509
But De Wiart could not give a heck about the
odds stacked against him and he just got on

00:15:48.509 --> 00:15:49.509
with it!

00:15:49.509 --> 00:15:53.709
His troops managed to break the encirclement
by traversing over the mountains and back

00:15:53.709 --> 00:15:57.999
to the coast, under a constant barrage of
artillery strikes and Luftwaffe attacks.

00:15:57.999 --> 00:16:01.350
Finally the Royal Navy came to the rescue
and managed to ferry De Wiart and his band

00:16:01.350 --> 00:16:05.980
of brothers out of Norway, landing in Great
Britain on his 60th birthday.

00:16:05.980 --> 00:16:11.019
In April 1941, Carton de Wiart was appointed
by Winston Churchill to lead a British secret

00:16:11.019 --> 00:16:12.730
mission in Yugoslavia.

00:16:12.730 --> 00:16:17.370
The objective was to make contact with Tito’s
partisans and offer them Allied support.

00:16:17.370 --> 00:16:18.620
But he never got there.

00:16:18.620 --> 00:16:22.129
After a refuelling stop in Malta, the Wellington
bomber in which the mission was travelling

00:16:22.129 --> 00:16:24.160
went into nosedive, straight into the Mediterranean.

00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:26.959
[fourth plane pings on screen]
Luckily a coastline was on sight.

00:16:26.959 --> 00:16:32.569
As the fuselage sank, De Wiart – 61, one-handed,
one-eyed – swam to shore, while carrying

00:16:32.569 --> 00:16:35.600
an injured comrade (!!!!!!!)
Alas, the coast they had reached, was the

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:40.600
coast of Libya, Axis territory, and Wiart
and his friends were immediately seized by

00:16:40.600 --> 00:16:41.600
the Italians.

00:16:41.600 --> 00:16:47.089
Our hero was flown by the Italian Military
to the high security POW camp established

00:16:47.089 --> 00:16:51.029
within the castle of Vincigliata [Veen-chee-llya-tah],
near Florence.

00:16:51.029 --> 00:16:55.129
This prison was specifically dedicated to
guarding senior British officers captured

00:16:55.129 --> 00:16:59.459
in the North Africa campaign[TA8] . The average
age of the prisoners was 52, but these indomitable

00:16:59.459 --> 00:17:03.050
officers had the energy of young soldiers
when it came to be breaking out.

00:17:03.050 --> 00:17:07.670
De Wiart was part of at least five escape
attempts, one of which involved digging a

00:17:07.670 --> 00:17:10.760
60-foot tunnel under the castle walls.

00:17:10.760 --> 00:17:12.970
A complicated endeavour for an amputee.

00:17:12.970 --> 00:17:18.030
But De Wiart could not give a damn and he
just got on with it!

00:17:18.030 --> 00:17:19.730
After seven months the tunnel was complete.

00:17:19.730 --> 00:17:23.350
De Wiart and five other prisoners succeeded
in their Great Escape in March 1943 and hid

00:17:23.350 --> 00:17:25.700
for some days in the countryside.

00:17:25.700 --> 00:17:30.450
Their cunning plan was to make themselves
inconspicuous by dressing like Italian peasants.

00:17:30.450 --> 00:17:35.330
Yeah, about that: considering that
one of them was a one-handed 63 year old gentleman

00:17:35.330 --> 00:17:39.130
with an eye patch, covered in scars, and
none of them could speak Italian

00:17:39.130 --> 00:17:43.640
… it was a miracle that they escaped capture
for eight days.

00:17:43.640 --> 00:17:46.160
De Wiart was soon back in captivity at Vincigliata.

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:48.070
Fun fact: this castle nowadays can be hired
for weddings!

00:17:48.070 --> 00:17:51.510
[Subtitle on screen: Escape tunnel available
for grooms with cold feet]

00:17:51.510 --> 00:17:53.740
But freedom was on the horizon.

00:17:53.740 --> 00:17:58.560
On the 25th of July 1943 Benito Mussolini
was ousted by a monarchist coup.

00:17:58.560 --> 00:18:04.310
King Victor Emmanuel III appointed Field Marshal
Badoglio as his successor, and the two conspirators

00:18:04.310 --> 00:18:08.890
continued honouring the alliance with Germany
– but only on the surface.

00:18:08.890 --> 00:18:12.130
Behind the scenes, they were looking to negotiate
an armistice with the Allies.

00:18:12.130 --> 00:18:16.820
They needed an intermediary and De Wiart seemed
to be the perfect choice, as he was due for

00:18:16.820 --> 00:18:19.220
release from the Castle anyways because of
his disability.

00:18:19.220 --> 00:18:23.560
De Wiart was taken to a tailor in Rome so
that he could get some civilian clothes.

00:18:23.560 --> 00:18:28.010
As a loyal customer of Savile Row he did not
trust Italian tailors and he protested that

00:18:28.010 --> 00:18:30.410
he didn’t want to “look like a gigolo”.

00:18:30.410 --> 00:18:33.760
But in the end, he was happy with the suit
they fashioned for him and on the 16th of

00:18:33.760 --> 00:18:39.160
August 1943 he was flown in secret to Lisbon
alongside General Castellano, the King’s

00:18:39.160 --> 00:18:40.320
chief negotiator.

00:18:40.320 --> 00:18:44.610
[TA9] After an agreement was reached, De Wiart
returned to the UK on the 28th of August.

00:18:44.610 --> 00:18:49.330
But he only had the chance to rest for one
month before Churchill personally appointed

00:18:49.330 --> 00:18:52.950
him as his special representative to the Chinese
Nationalist leader Chiang Kai-Shek.

00:18:52.950 --> 00:19:02.070
Rest at last
Compared to the swash and buckle of the previous

00:19:02.070 --> 00:19:06.800
years, De Wiart’s stay in China was relatively
calm, as his was more of diplomatic mission,

00:19:06.800 --> 00:19:08.040
rather than a military one.

00:19:08.040 --> 00:19:11.860
During this time, while advising Chiang on
his strategies against the Japanese, he befriended

00:19:11.860 --> 00:19:16.550
him and his wife, but when he got to meet
Mao, well, he simply could not stand him.

00:19:16.550 --> 00:19:20.930
The Generalissimo Chiang Kai-Shek and Mao
Tse Tung were uneasy allies in the war against

00:19:20.930 --> 00:19:25.520
Japan, but De Wiart was a staunch anti-communist
and did not trust the future leader of China.

00:19:25.520 --> 00:19:28.760
During a formal dinner, Mao delivered a lengthy
speech on the qualities of his faction.

00:19:28.760 --> 00:19:33.430
When he mentioned how hard they were fighting
the Japanese, De Wiart cut him short.

00:19:33.430 --> 00:19:38.120
“He tried my credulity a little too far
… I told him that what they were doing was

00:19:38.120 --> 00:19:42.410
to keep looking over their shoulder to see
what the Generalissimo was doing first!”

00:19:42.410 --> 00:19:45.910
To his surprise, Mao took no offence and simply
laughed.

00:19:45.910 --> 00:19:48.970
Another leader asked him why he hadn’t gone
to Yenan to see their operations.

00:19:48.970 --> 00:19:51.670
“I answered quite frankly that I hated Communism.”

00:19:51.670 --> 00:19:56.600
De Wiart stayed in China until 1947, surviving
yet another plane crash along the way.

00:19:56.600 --> 00:19:57.600
[plane nr 5!]

00:19:57.600 --> 00:19:58.620
And let’s add another notch, shall we?

00:19:58.620 --> 00:20:03.640
On the way back to Britain De Wiart paid visit
at a friend’s house in Rangoon, Burma.

00:20:03.640 --> 00:20:08.520
While walking down a flight of stairs he was
ambushed by an unpredictable foe.

00:20:08.520 --> 00:20:09.520
A coconut mat.

00:20:09.520 --> 00:20:13.060
He slipped on it and went flying down the
stairs, breaking a couple of vertebrae along

00:20:13.060 --> 00:20:14.060
the way.

00:20:14.060 --> 00:20:15.240
[Injury tally pings for the last time]
The good thing is that the doctors treating

00:20:15.240 --> 00:20:18.950
his back took the occasion to remove quite
a bit of old shrapnel from his body.

00:20:18.950 --> 00:20:22.210
It was now about time to retire for good from
active duty.

00:20:22.210 --> 00:20:25.170
General Carton De Wiart moved to a family
estate in Killinardrish, Ireland and spent

00:20:25.170 --> 00:20:30.550
the next years writing his memoirs, hunting,
fishing and polishing his collection of more

00:20:30.550 --> 00:20:32.130
than 30 military medals.

00:20:32.130 --> 00:20:34.160
Eventually even such a legend had to die.

00:20:34.160 --> 00:20:39.690
After the last few peaceful years Adrian Carton
de Wiart’s life came to an end on the 5th

00:20:39.690 --> 00:20:42.640
of June 1963, in his Irish home.

00:20:42.640 --> 00:20:46.170
While in recent years his achievements have
been rediscovered and celebrated by major

00:20:46.170 --> 00:20:50.330
media outlets, this inspiring character is
still little known, surprisingly.

00:20:50.330 --> 00:20:56.620
I hope we at Biographics did our bit to perpetuate
his memory and we hope you could do the same

00:20:56.620 --> 00:20:57.620
and share this video.

00:20:57.620 --> 00:21:01.140
Frankly, I have enjoyed the story of this
gentleman and I hope you did, too.

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:04.750
Before leaving, please answer this question
in the comments: if you were to shoot a movie

00:21:04.750 --> 00:21:16.580
about Carton De Wiart, who would you like
to see in the lead role?

